{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs",
  "item": "impl[CachedProvider/SourceProvider]/fn[read]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "The provider overrides parse with a cached snapshot. Selected-source\n reads must deliver and fingerprint that snapshot without fetching again;\n this trait-required callback rejects a refetch. Primitive provider reads\n are covered separately, and executing this guard would fail this test."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tests.rs#impl[CachedProvider/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` meets the built-in's criteria.\n\n- **Placement and form:** The citation is on an explicit function with a body. The explanation is in fenced citation commentary inside the assessed item, so it counts as a tracked justification.\n- **The reason is substantive and matches the source:**\n  - The sibling `impl[CachedProvider/SourceProvider]/fn[parse]` overrides `parse` and returns a cached snapshot through `parse_snapshot`. The default `parse` in `trait[SourceProvider]/fn[parse]` is the path that would call `read`, and the override bypasses it.\n  - `fn[read]` is a trait-required callback whose body only returns `Err(SourceError::Invalid(\"must not refetch\"))`.\n  - The test `fn[cached_selected_source_reads_record_exact_delivered_contents_and_errors_add_no_input]` asserts that `ctx.ask::<bool>()` succeeds and that the recorded dependency fingerprint is the snapshot text. Running the guard would therefore fail the test, so zero execution is expected.\n- **The claim that primitive reads are covered elsewhere holds:** Other test providers in the crate exercise `read` directly, namely `impl[Provider/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` in `agent/tests.rs` and `impl[Primitives/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` and `impl[Memory/SourceProvider]/fn[read]` in `sources/tests.rs`.\n- **No redundancy:** The item has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the same target, so the citation is not redundant.",
